Title: The Letter in the Tent
Order: 11
Production Code: 1x11

INT. EXPEDITION CAMP - REUEL'S TENT - NIGHT

AMB: a lion calling somewhere on the plain

Professor Stone and Reuel Briggs talk late over pipes about Meroe's ancient wealth. Charlie Vance half-listens from his bedroll; Jim Titus sits apart, reading a letter by candlelight.

PROFESSOR STONE
It is a fact that Egypt drew from Ethiopia all the arts, sciences and knowledge of which she was mistress. The very soil of Egypt was pilfered by the Nile from the foundations of Meroe.

REUEL BRIGGS
(a hidden note of sarcasm)
How can the Anglo-Saxon world bear the establishment of such a theory?

CHARLIE VANCE
(from his bed)
Gee whiz! Count me out!

Stone retires. Reuel makes notes in his journal — and a rush of balmy air passes over his brow. A dim shadow falls across the tent door: the handsome Negress who appeared to Dianthe and signed herself "Mira."

Behind Reuel, unseen by him directly, Jim Titus unfolds a letter from an inner pocket. The shadow's finger traces the page, letter by letter, and Reuel reads it as clearly as if it were his own.

TITUS'S LETTER (V.O.)
(Aubrey's handwriting)
Use your discretion about the final act. Be sure the letters are destroyed. Address me at Laurel Hill — Thomas Johnson. A.L.

The vision passes. Reuel sits frozen, the leopard's slowness to be helped suddenly making an entirely different kind of sense.

Later, a wild, shrill cry jolts him out of a doze: "Reuel, Reuel, save me!" — three times, clear and close beside his ear.

REUEL BRIGGS
Charlie — Charlie, wake up and listen!

At first Charlie hears only the lion. Then both of them hear it: a prolonged wail rising above the lion's call, and the words "Charlie, brother, save me!" After several tries, Reuel gathers his powers and slips into the clairvoyant trance, rigid and statuesque, while Charlie listens.

REUEL BRIGGS (CONT'D)
(in trance)
There is Molly, poor Molly. And your father weeps, and the friends are there and they too weep — but where is my own sweet girl, Dianthe? I cannot see her. I do not find the poor maimed body of my love. And Aubrey! Traitor, false friend! I shall return for vengeance. Wake me, Charlie.

A few upward passes of Charlie's hands, and Reuel wakes.
